\chapter{High-Flux Thermodynamics}

\section{Overview}
The Matter Jet Mk-30 operates at a continuous power envelope of
\[
P_0 = 1.0 \times 10^{16}~\text{W},
\]
with a conversion efficiency of $\eta = 0.95$.  
This implies a waste-heat load of
\[
Q_{\text{waste}} = (1 - \eta) P_0 = 5.0 \times 10^{14}~\text{W},
\]
which must be extracted continuously to maintain thermodynamic stability.

This chapter develops the full high-flux thermodynamic framework governing:
\begin{itemize}
\item GaIn micro-channel cooling,
\item CNT radiator arrays,
\item thermal shock limits,
\item radiation spectrum analysis,
\item enthalpy routing,
\item structural thermal resilience.
\end{itemize}

\section{Waste Heat Generation}
The relativistic jet engine converts electromagnetic energy into kinetic jet
energy with efficiency $\eta$. The remaining fraction is thermalized within the
reaction core and must be removed.

The waste heat is
\begin{equation}
Q_{\text{waste}} = (1 - \eta) P_0.
\end{equation}

For the Mk-30,
\[
Q_{\text{waste}} = 5.0 \times 10^{14}~\text{W}.
\]

\section{Thermal Radiation Flux}
The core plasma radiates according to
\begin{equation}
F_{\text{rad}} = \sigma T^4,
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant.

For $T = 1.42 \times 10^8$ K,
\[
F_{\text{rad}} \approx 1.2 \times 10^{24}~\text{W/m}^2.
\]

This flux is confined by the W-Re liner and extracted by the cooling system.

\section{GaIn Micro-Channel Cooling}
The GaIn coolant absorbs heat according to
\begin{equation}
Q = \dot{m}_{\text{coolant}} c_p \Delta T.
\end{equation}

The Mk-30 coolant loop achieves:
\[
\Delta T = 412.4~\text{K}.
\]

The micro-channel geometry maximizes:
\begin{itemize}
\item surface area,
\item convective heat transfer,
\item thermal uniformity,
\item coolant velocity.
\end{itemize}

\section{Micro-Channel Geometry}
The micro-channel heat exchanger consists of:
\begin{itemize}
\item etched GaIn channels,
\item SiC structural backing,
\item W-Re thermal interface layer,
\item CNT radiator coupling.
\end{itemize}

The heat transfer coefficient is
\begin{equation}
h_{\text{channel}} = \frac{Q}{A \Delta T}.
\end{equation}

For the Mk-30,
\[
h_{\text{channel}} \approx 1.8 \times 10^7~\text{W/m}^2\text{K}.
\]

\section{CNT Radiator Arrays}
The CNT radiator arrays operate at
\[
T_{\text{eff}} \approx 5450~\text{K}.
\]

The radiated power is
\begin{equation}
P_{\text{rad}} = \epsilon \sigma A T_{\text{eff}}^4,
\end{equation}
with emissivity $\epsilon \approx 1$.

The spectrum peaks at
\[
\lambda_{\text{peak}} = \frac{2.898 \times 10^{-3}}{T_{\text{eff}}}
\approx 531~\text{nm},
\]
matching solar photosphere emission.

\section{Thermal Shock Limits}
Thermal shock stress is
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{\text{shock}} = E \alpha \Delta T,
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $E$ is Young’s modulus,
\item $\alpha$ is thermal expansion coefficient.
\end{itemize}

SiC and W-Re remain within safe limits for:
\[
\Delta T < 500~\text{K}.
\]

\section{Thermal Expansion and Structural Integrity}
The thermal expansion is
\begin{equation}
\Delta L = \alpha L \Delta T.
\end{equation}

The Mk-30 structural materials exhibit:
\begin{itemize}
\item low thermal expansion,
\item high thermal conductivity,
\item high melting points,
\item radiation resistance.
\end{itemize}

\section{Radiation Energy Density}
The radiation energy density is
\begin{equation}
u_{\text{rad}} = a T^4,
\end{equation}
where $a$ is the radiation constant.

For the Mk-30,
\[
u_{\text{rad}} \approx 4.1 \times 10^{25}~\text{J/m}^3.
\]

\section{Thermal Routing and Enthalpy Flow}
The enthalpy flux is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{H} = h \gamma^2 \mathbf{v},
\end{equation}
where $h$ is enthalpy density.

Relativistic enthalpy routing prevents thermalization at solid boundaries.

\section{Thermal Stability Criteria}
Thermal stability requires:
\begin{itemize}
\item continuous coolant flow,
\item uniform micro-channel distribution,
\item CNT radiator emission,
\item structural thermal resilience,
\item real-time thermal telemetry.
\end{itemize}

The Mk-30 satisfies all criteria.

\section{Summary}
The Mk-30 high-flux thermodynamic system provides:
\begin{itemize}
\item 500~TW waste heat absorption,
\item GaIn micro-channel cooling,
\item CNT radiator emission at 5450~K,
\item thermal shock resilience,
\item relativistic enthalpy routing.
\end{itemize}

These properties enable continuous 10~PW operation and support the hardware
integration developed in Chapter 7.

